Article 6 (Ownership and Open-Source Components)
   The source code of the Software is not published, and all rights, including intellectual property rights, belong to the Developer.
   Open-source components bundled with the Software are governed by their own licenses, not by these Terms, and these Terms do not limit your rights under those licenses. The installer includes third-party open-source programs such as FFmpeg (an LGPL-3.0 build in which the license provider changed one Direct3D 11-related part), yt-dlp and Node.js. They run as separate programs, are governed by their own licenses, and these Terms do not restrict the rights those licenses give you (copying, modifying, replacing with a build you made yourself, reverse engineering and so on). The component list and full license texts are in the open-source notices and in the licenses folder of the installation. The complete source, patch and build instructions for the modified FFmpeg are at https://github.com/sangre72/ffmpeg/tree/n8.1.3-17-d3d11fix , and the Developer will provide the source on request through the contact address.

Article 7 (Third-Party Services and Content)
   - When you use the relevant feature, the Software installed on your PC connects directly from your PC to these third-party services: checking for and downloading new versions of the bundled open-source tool yt-dlp (GitHub), fetching the internet radio station list (Radio Browser — only when you press "Get radio channels"), listening to radio (each station’s broadcast server), album information and cover art (MusicBrainz, Cover Art Archive), lyrics (LRCLIB), public IP detection (ipify and similar IP lookup services), certificate issuance (Let’s Encrypt), and payment and license verification (the Payment Provider). These connections do not pass through any Developer system.
   - Content such as radio and My Media belongs to its respective rights holders. All connecting, playing, converting and storing happens solely on your own PC. The Developer and the Developer’s systems and facilities do not provide, hold, store, relay, cache, transmit or process third-party content, and no Developer system is a point through which that content passes or is stored. The Developer grants no rights in third-party content.
   - The media-access features of the Software are only a one-to-one personal playback connection between your own PC and your own devices (car, phone); they are not public redistribution or broadcasting to an unspecified audience. The Developer respects the legitimate rights of content owners. Accordingly, if a third-party service or its rights holder so requests, or where necessary to comply with law or a service’s terms, the Developer may suspend, restrict or block all or part of the personal playback connection for that service without prior notice. This is not meant to restrict your rights unfairly but to keep a lawful and sustainable environment. Any suspension, restriction or block is carried out only through a software update or a feature-setting change, and in doing so the Developer does not view or collect your content or playback history.
   - Track information: MusicBrainz (musicbrainz.org). Release data comes from the MusicBrainz database, which is published under CC0 (public domain).
   - Album art: Cover Art Archive (coverartarchive.org, operated by MusicBrainz and the Internet Archive). Copyright in cover images belongs to their rights holders. The Software downloads and caches on your PC the cover for a track playing on your PC only to display it on screen, and does not redistribute it.
   - Lyrics: LRCLIB (lrclib.net). Copyright in lyrics belongs to their rights holders. The Software downloads the lyrics of the track being played and stores them on your PC only to display them on screen, and does not redistribute them.
   - These services are third-party services unrelated to the Developer, and their information may be missing or wrong. If a music file contains its own information (tags, artwork, .lrc lyrics), that is used first.
   - Radio: the Software provides only a radio player. On first install the station list is empty and no stations are pre-loaded. Only when you choose a country and press "Get radio channels" does your PC fetch, from a public station directory (Radio Browser), the stream addresses that stations have published, and save them on your PC (in the data folder of the installation) — an address-collection aid for you. The list stays on that PC and is not fetched automatically when you open the tab. Only public addresses that anyone can listen to are fetched; addresses needing a login or authentication token, or that could not be verified to work, are not fetched. You decide which country’s list to fetch and which stations to listen to or hide. The broadcast is received by your PC directly from each station’s server, passed through the sound settings (volume leveling, effects) on that PC, and sent only to your own registered car or phone. This delivery takes place only between your PC and your own devices and does not pass through any Developer system. The Developer does not provide, store or forward broadcasts and is not the owner or redistributor of any broadcast. Rights in the broadcasts belong to each station. If a station changes its address or stops transmitting, that station may become unavailable, and names and quality information in the list are exactly as written in the public directory.
   - In plain words: the app is just a player that plays radio, and the broadcasts belong to the stations. The app only helps collect addresses that are publicly available on the internet; you choose what to fetch and listen to. Broadcasts can change or drop because of the stations.
   - My Media: this feature plays music and video files that you yourself have put in the Media folder (inside the installation folder) of the PC where Colibre is installed, sending them from that PC to your registered car or phone. Files are not uploaded anywhere, and there is no feature to upload files from a phone or car to the PC. The Developer provides no content (the Demo test files included at install are an exception; their source and license are in CREDITS.txt in the Demo folder). The files you add are yours; you must have the legitimate right to use them (for example copies you bought or lawfully own) and use them for personal purposes only. Do not use this app for public screenings or to share or redistribute them. The Developer does not inspect or keep your files.
   - In plain words: this is a feature for watching your own files, which you put on your PC, in your car or on your phone. The files never leave your PC, and the Developer does not look at or take them. The files must be ones you legitimately own, and are for personal viewing only.
   - Responsibility for complying with copyright law and each service’s terms of use rests with you. If a problem arises or something seems to conflict with terms or law, stop using the feature immediately.
   - Some features may stop working without notice because of changes at third-party services.

